I have a Linux firewall machine (coyote linux) with 2 ethernet cards, 1 with
public IPv4 addr, 1 internal private addr.  I am trying to get this machine
to masquerade IPv6-over-IPv4 configured tunnel packets (protocol 41).

I have the ipchains rules set up:

ipchains -A input -i $PRIVATE_IF -p 41 -s $INT_TUN_END -d $EXT_TUN_END -j
ACCEPT -l
ipchains -A forward -i $PUBLIC_IF -p 41 -s $INT_TUN_END -d $EXT_TUN_END -j
MASQ -l
ipchains -A output -i $PUBLIC_IF -p 41 -d $EXT_TUN_END -j ACCEPT -l
ipchains -A input -i $PUBLIC_IF -p 41 -s $EXT_TUN_END -j ACCEPT -l
ipchains -A output -i $PRIVATE_IF -p 41 -d $INT_TUN_END -j ACCEPT -l

When I do a IPv6 ping from the internal tunnel endpoint machine, I can see
the traffic arrive in the firewall logs:

Sep 23 13:05:27 (none) kernel: Packet log: input ACCEPT eth0 PROTO=41
192.168.0.2:65535 147.32.120.55:65535 L=120 S=0x00 I=62 F=0x0000 T=255 (#34)

Sep 23 13:05:27 (none) kernel: Packet log: forward MASQ eth1 PROTO=41
192.168.0.2:65535 147.32.120.55:65535 L=120 S=0x00 I=62 F=0x0000 T=254 (#8) 

So, the tunnel packets are traversing the input and forward chains OK, but
they never seem to appear on the output chain.

Does this mean that the MASQ is failing somehow?

Is ipchains supposed to support masquerading for tunnelled packets, or does
it only work with TCP and UDP?

The firewall machine is IPv4 only, but I assume it doesn't need to know
about v6 at all as all it needs to do is change the v4 header to masq.

Thanks in advance for any help.

